<h2>Step 2: Execution on the bike</h2>

<p>The bike is where triathlon nutrition is won or lost. It is the segment where you can consume the most calories because there is no mechanical impact bouncing your stomach.</p>

<p>Our goal is to push intake up to 120 g/h. To achieve this without collapsing the digestive system on an extremely hot day, your fluid logistics must be flawless.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>The athlete carries the powder, the course provides the water.</strong> Your bottles should carry the concentrated carbohydrate powder mixture. You take plain water from the aid stations to dilute and drink.</li>
  <li><strong>Adjusting dilution for heat:</strong> If you normally need 750 ml (25 oz) of water to process 100 g of carbohydrates, on a hot day you will need over a liter (34 oz) for those same 100 g. Your water requirement goes up due to sweat, but your gastric emptying capacity remains limited.</li>
  <li><strong>The decision to reduce:</strong> If you feel your stomach stop and water sloshing as you pedal, pause your carbohydrate intake temporarily. Drink only plain water until your stomach empties. It is better to lower your average to 90 g/h and assimilate it, than to push 120 g/h and throw up.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Step 3: Transition and the run</h2>

<p>Running in the heat is where physical impact compounds with accumulated dehydration. The gastrointestinal tract is already stressed from the bike. Here, the absolute ceiling is 60 g/h, typically executed with one gel every 20 minutes.</p>

<p>Sodium plays a crucial role. The baseline is to consume about 200 mg of sodium every 40 minutes. However, this is the variable that changes most with the weather. In cold climates (like a triathlon in Bogotá), you may need less. In hot and humid conditions, that figure must scale with your sweat rate to prevent hyponatremia and maintain blood volume, which in turn helps irrigate the stomach.</p>

<div class="datanote">
  <p><strong>The caffeine protocol:</strong> We use caffeine strategically. 200 mg before the start and 200 mg in T1 (bike-to-run transition). This puts about 300 mg active in your system during the run. Caffeine reduces perceived exertion, helping you maintain form when heat exhaustion hits. <em>Note: Train this dose beforehand starting from 100 mg intakes, as it depends on body weight and individual tolerance.</em></p>
</div>

<h2>Step 4: The 72-hour defense</h2>

<p>You can execute the perfect pace and exact hydration, and still have stomach issues if you reach the start line with a gut full of fiber. The heat merely accelerates a problem that brewed three days prior.</p>

<p>Carbohydrate loading is not a license to eat pizza. It is a mathematical, methodical protocol that starts 48 to 72 hours before the gun goes off.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Eliminate fiber from day -3.</strong> No raw vegetables, no fruit with skin, no whole grains. The goal is to empty the intestine of residue.</li>
  <li><strong>Target 10 g/kg of body weight.</strong> Between 1 and 1.5 days before the race, your intake should reach up to 10 grams of white, low-fiber carbohydrates per kilogram of body weight. White rice, classic pasta, white bread. If it is your first time doing a strict load, start at 5 g/kg and scale up in future races.</li>
  <li><strong>Lean protein.</strong> Chicken or eggs. Fats should stay between 40 and 80 g daily because they slow down digestion—something you do not want close to the event.</li>
</ul>

<p>If you arrive at the race with full glycogen stores and an empty intestine, you remove a massive burden from your digestive system. When the heat tries to shut down your stomach, it will not have to fight a salad from the night before.</p>

